WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.240
- In the wake of the mass hysteria,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.330
that was the January 6th
riots at the US Capitol,

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.910
a lot of people felt something
anything had to be done

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.730
and social media was the
immediate first target,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.220
first President Trump was banned

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.000
from using Facebook and Twitter,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.620
then Google and Apple made
the decision to remove Parler

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.040
from their app stores,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.790
some billed Parler as a
bastion of free speech

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:22.800
and light moderation,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.470
others said it would cater
to conservative voices,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.870
others just said it was
filled with hate speech

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.810
but the companies justified their actions

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.200
in removing Parler by
noting that Parler was used,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.420
to both insight and organize events,

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.970
that unfolded at the
Capitol on January 6th.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.980
- What else was I supposed to do,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.110
the guy I wanted to win didn't win?

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.340
- And it all came to a head,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.910
when Amazon decided to
suspend Parler's access,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.690
to their web services platform
in taking down this harmless,

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.170
right-wing alternative to Facebook,

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.920
millions of individuals were
left with no means of sharing,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.780
their legitimate political views or plans,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.440
to organize peaceful protests,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.950
without the oversight and intrusion

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.690
of Facebook and Twitter's
clearly biased fact-checkers

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.870
and haters of free speech,

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.363
at least that's what some
people want you to believe.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:10.440
- [Narrator] Brought
to you by Mack Weldon.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.970
- Hey, Legal Eagles it's time to think,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.860
like a corporate lawyer
because Parler is suing Amazon

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.230
for getting kicked off of the AWS platform

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.810
but what was the basis for Amazon,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.180
to abruptly suspend the
use of their web services

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.560
by this ultra conservative Twitter clone?

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.210
Did they break their agreement
with Parler or even worse

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.530
is there a conspiracy
between Amazon and Twitter,

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.010
to nuke the competition

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.100
and what does this all have to
do with the first amendment?

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.590
And let's talk about the public version

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.660
of why Parler got suspended?

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.280
Amazon and Parler had a contract,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.070
detailing each party's
rights and responsibilities,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.670
Parler was allowed to use
Amazon as its web host

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.050
but only if it agreed to
follow some basic rules,

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.980
including moderating content,

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.170
so, why did Amazon suspend
Parler's web services?

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.520
Well, both companies agree,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.560
that Parler's lacks moderation policies,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.420
have been an ongoing source of conflict

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.710
between these two companies,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.770
since at least November of last year,

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.000
now, all internet hosting
companies have rules of their own,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.450
about what content is and is not allowed,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.890
Amazon and Twitter seemed to
have more stringent standards,

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.310
about what content gets posted
to their website platforms,

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.950
Parler on the other hand takes
a more complicated approach,

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.360
to handling complaints,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.310
Parler relied on juries of
its own users to determine,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.020
whether an offensive
post broke its own rules,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.040
crowdsourcing adjudication of complaints,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.930
proved to be really slow, how slow?

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.200
Well, at the time that
Amazon booted Parler,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.710
there was a backlog of 26,000 complaints,

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.000
that Parler juries had yet to adjudicate

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.260
and on November 17th, 2020,

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.420
seven weeks before the
insurrectionist stormed the Capitol,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.270
Amazon emailed Parler to highlight
some of the violent posts

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.140
that were made on its services,

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.200
one post derided Michelle
Obama using racist words

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.100
in response another user said,

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.690
the only good Democrat is
a dead one kill them all,

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.900
a representative of Parler
replied to the post,

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.150
hateful as it is would not be deemed,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.750
a violation of our terms and service,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.100
Amazon essentially told
Parler that this was a problem

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.900
because Parler either did not care to

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.440
or they simply did not
have an effective way,

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.000
to comply with the Amazon
web service company's,

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.300
terms of service including
moderating violent rhetoric,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.360
the issue came to a head,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.990
after the deadly riots of the Capitol,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.260
when violent and threatening posts,

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.710
continued to go unchecked by Parler,

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:21.130
despite multiple parlays
between Amazon and Parler,

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.300
the violent posts increased
with only half-hearted responses

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.320
being provided by Parler to increase,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.860
their moderation efforts
by using more volunteers

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:29.947
and on January 9th,

00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:32.520
Parler was notified by
Amazon Web Services,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.210
that at 11:59 PM on Sunday January 10th,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.030
they would stop providing web services

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.580
for the website and app

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.230
for a week Parler services
were completely down

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.440
with only recent signs of reappearing,

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.940
now when Apple and Google
removed the Parler app

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.430
from their respective app stores,

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.970
no one could download the app
but the data was still there

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.680
and it could be migrated elsewhere

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.390
but with Amazon going down

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.010
not only could Parler not obtain new users

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:58.410
but the users they currently had,

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.130
could no longer use
Parler at all as a result,

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:04.440
Parler filed a lawsuit in
federal court on January 11th,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.130
seeking a temporary restraining
order or TRO against Amazon

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.590
in the hopes of either preventing
Amazon from removing them

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.850
from their web services
or if that went through,

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.570
putting them back on the AWS platform,

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:19.310
so, let's dig into this lawsuit
starting with the complaints

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:21.660
that Parler filed against Amazon,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.770
it contains three causes of
action or court thingies,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.290
that it says that Amazon has done wrong

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.180
and what's as interesting as
the three causes of action,

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.410
that are included are the
ones that are not included,

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.410
there is nothing in this complaint,

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.420
about the first amendment, why?

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.480
Because that's not actually a thing

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.110
as we've talked about many
times on this channel,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.200
the first amendment doesn't
apply to individual people,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.310
citizens or private companies
it stops the government

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.100
from restricting or mandating
speech of its citizens,

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.450
so as a result Parler doesn't
include a cause of action,

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.080
against Amazon under the first amendment

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.050
because that's not really a thing

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.680
but the first cause of
action that Parler search,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.050
relates to the Sherman Antitrust Act

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.250
and the Sherman Antitrust Act
is just about as misunderstood

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.290
as the first amendment but
at least the Antitrust Act

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.020
is a real legal thing that
you are allowed to claim

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.310
and Parler claims that Amazon Web Services

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:16.710
is in cahoots with Twitter

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.880
and conspired to de-platform Parler,

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.170
to effectively remove
them as a competitor,

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.400
Parler claims this violate section one

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.000
of the Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.880
the federal statute which
prohibits activities,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.320
that restrict interstate
commerce and competition

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.620
in the marketplace

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.540
and here's the big thing that
most people don't understand

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.030
about the Sherman Antitrust Act

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:36.820
and Antitrust laws in general
which is that in the US,

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.440
it's not illegal to be
a really big company.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.630
- You see GE owns kitchen all of Colorado,

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.520
which in turn owns JMI of Stanford,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.100
which is a majority shareholder
in pokerfastlane.com,

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.110
which recently acquired
the Sheinhardt wig company,

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.420
which owns NBC outright, NBC
owns Winnipeg Iron works,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.650
which owns the AHP Chanagi
Party Meats corporation

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:56.020
of Pyongyang, North Korea.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.740
- It's not even illegal to
be a monopoly or monopsony,

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.620
that alone is not illegal

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.750
but what is illegal under anti-trust laws

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.870
is using your market powers
to get an unfair advantage

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.600
and the number one thing you can't do,

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.760
the reason antitrust laws
existed in the first place

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.550
is conspire with a bunch
of other companies,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.440
to root out the competition
that's what a cartel is,

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.810
it's a group of companies
who conspire together

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.450
for an unfair advantage

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.410
and there are a bunch of
other improper things,

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:25.590
that are made illegal by
the Sherman Antitrust Act

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.810
but what's relevant here is section one

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.410
and in order to prove a violation

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:31.150
of section one of the
Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.910
basically requires proof of one,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.080
the existence of an actual conspiracy,

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.890
two, intention on the part
of those co-conspirators,

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.950
to restrain trade and three
actual injury to the competition

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.190
and to support this claim,

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.650
Parler alleges the
following facts number one,

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.200
Amazon Web Services and Twitter,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:48.770
signed a multi-year agreement

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:53.000
for Twitter's use of Amazon Web
Services in December of 2020

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.680
two on January 8th, Twitter
banned president Trump,

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.010
giving an opportunity for Parler,

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:59.330
to gain a large number of
disgruntled Twitter users,

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.680
including Trump himself and three,

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.060
less than 24 hours after
Trump's Twitter ban,

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.190
Amazon indefinitely banned Parler

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:07.910
from using its web services,

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.980
Amazon's response was
that these allegations,

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.940
don't meet the basic threshold requirement

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.790
for a Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:16.270
now, the Supreme court
has long held a complaint,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.740
alleging a violation of
the Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.267
must provide "enough factual
matter taken as true,

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:23.450
"to suggest that an agreement was made",

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:26.580
however, as Amazon pointed
out in responding briefs,

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.800
Parler does not even claim
that Twitter and AWS,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.300
communicated about Parler
much less formed an agreement

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.360
and in fact Parler didn't
present any evidence,

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.630
direct or circumstantial,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.890
that shows that there
was an actual conspiracy

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.470
or intent on the part of Twitter

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:42.290
and Amazon to restrain Parler's business,

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.520
now, conspiracy doesn't require,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.300
an explicit contract between the two

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.050
but Parler doesn't actually
provide any evidence,

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:51.830
any agreement or meeting of the minds,

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.110
no matter how informal was actually made

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:56.680
or that any communications
took place whatsoever,

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.810
moreover the Supreme court
has explicitly held that

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.330
if a business has an independent
business justification

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.090
for its conduct and there's no basis

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:06.260
for rejecting that justification
as legitimate explanation,

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.440
the case will be dismissed for example,

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.650
when Sprint was sued
for allegedly colluding

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:13.350
with other carriers to
raise text message prices

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.500
a court dismissed the claim,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:16.340
even though text messaging prices,

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.180
went up throughout the entire
telecommunications industry,

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.850
Sprint had or claimed to have,

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.350
an independent business justification

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.380
for raising per message prices,

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.350
the company wanted to
push more subscribers,

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.730
to purchase bundled
calling and text plans,

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.070
the court found that this was
a legitimate justification

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.230
and one that all the
telecom companies shared,

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:36.000
a desire to push people toward
buying bundles services,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.160
there wasn't any proof that Sprint,

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:38.270
even played a leadership role

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:39.780
in increasing per message pricing

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.880
nor was there enough evidence to show,

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.660
that the telecom companies
colluded amongst each other,

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.400
to do everything in concert

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.750
and here Parler alleges
that Amazon's motivation

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:50.520
for taking its action was political

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:52.790
and that it had no
legitimate business reasons

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.190
for terminating the agreement

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:56.860
and Parler goes back
towards the conspiracy idea,

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:00.090
asserting that Amazon
wanted to benefit Twitter

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.290
but Parler didn't make
a single allegation,

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:03.410
that conversations were held,

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.600
between Twitter and
Amazon regarding Parler,

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.740
let alone they had some
master plan in place,

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.220
to jointly stifle Twitter's competition

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:12.730
and even if Amazon had acted unilaterally

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.050
for the benefit of Twitter,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.950
that still would not be enough
for an antitrust action,

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.070
again, without the conspiracy
element being satisfied,

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.760
an allegation of violating
the Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.280
falls on its face of course
what was more plausible,

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:26.760
between Amazon and Twitter

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.200
and frankly Apple and
Google at the same time

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.530
is something referred to
as conscious parallelism,

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.450
conscious parallelism is
the undisclosed imitation

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.360
of another business action,

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:37.970
this includes things like gas stations,

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:39.270
across the street from each other,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:40.740
mimicking each other's prices

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.860
or telecoms all raising
the price of text messages,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:45.170
the same was likely the case

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.010
with the wave of responses to Parler

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.430
in the wake of the Capitol
rights as one company realized,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.430
they needed to cut ties with this company,

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:54.640
others without discussion or conspiracy,

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.610
simply decided they needed to follow suit,

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:58.640
to both protect their business interests

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.790
and prevent future similar events,

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:03.360
basically, everyone realized
that Parler was toxic

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.020
at exactly the same time

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:07.890
because of everything that happened,

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.360
now that takes us to the
second cause of action

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.900
for breach of contract

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.800
and of course I saved the
breach of contract alligation

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:16.930
for the middle of the video
because as we all know,

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:20.650
contract law is the sexiest of all laws,

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:23.590
that's right I'm bringing sexy contract,

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.280
now the basic premise of
this allegation is twofold,

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:29.520
first that Amazon's suspension
was really a termination

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.100
for an alleged material breach

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:32.930
and second any termination for cause,

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:36.850
due to a purported material
breach required a 30 day notice

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.910
and precure period to fix the breaches,

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:41.560
which was not provided,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:43.110
now, let's just start
off by giving Parler,

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:44.330
the benefit of a doubt here,

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:47.440
that Amazon suspension was in
fact a de facto termination,

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:48.273
the clause of the agreement,

00:10:48.273 --> 00:10:50.487
that Parler hangs their
argument on states,

00:10:50.487 --> 00:10:53.097
"either party may terminate
this agreement for cause

00:10:53.097 --> 00:10:55.817
"if the other party is in
material breach of this agreement

00:10:55.817 --> 00:10:57.697
"and the material breach remains uncured

00:10:57.697 --> 00:11:00.087
"for a period of 30 days
from the receipt of notice,

00:11:00.087 --> 00:11:02.810
"by the other party," now,
unfortunately for Parler,

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:05.330
you can't pick and choose
what portion of the agreement,

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.680
makes your argument seem more plausible,

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.450
the customer agreement with Amazon,

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:12.330
also contains a broader right
to terminate an agreement,

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.680
without requiring a 30 day cure period

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.680
in that same section referenced
by Parler and its complaint,

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.620
the Amazon Web Services
customer agreement,

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:22.867
gives specific unilateral right to Amazon,

00:11:22.867 --> 00:11:25.537
"terminate this agreement
immediately upon notice to you

00:11:25.537 --> 00:11:29.367
"for cause if we have the right
to suspend under section six

00:11:29.367 --> 00:11:32.177
"and section six allows for
suspension for any conduct,

00:11:32.177 --> 00:11:34.297
"by the customer or its user,

00:11:34.297 --> 00:11:36.140
"that's in breach of the agreement,"

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:38.370
so, the whole premise of Parler's argument

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.430
is basically blown to pieces

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.070
as referenced in their complaint,

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:45.330
a breach of contract requires
the contract imposes a duty,

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.310
the duty is breached

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.540
and the breach proximately
causes damages to the claimant

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:50.720
in the present instance though,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.510
the alleged duty a 30 day
notice was a straw man of sorts,

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.340
it didn't matter if Amazon
gave them one day notice

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:58.620
or no notice at all,

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.720
per the terms of the
agreement that Parler signed,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.640
Amazon had a separate unilateral right,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:05.070
to terminate the agreement
immediately for conduct,

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.650
that was in breach of the
agreement no, at the same time,

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.530
a lot of people have looked
at this contract and said,

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:12.320
gee, this contract seems super one-sided,

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:14.500
it gives Amazon a ton of power

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:16.830
and it seems like they
can terminate anyone

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:20.120
for almost any reason
and people are right,

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.730
that's how these contracts
are often written

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:24.790
and companies are considered,

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:27.580
to be sophisticated business
players and they can contract,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.900
to basically whatever they want

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:31.030
as long as it's not against public policy

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:33.700
and here Amazon had a contract,

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:36.430
it's probably the same
contract uses for everyone,

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:38.490
that hosts web content with them

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:41.650
and Parler had the option
not to sign the contract

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.650
but they chose to and
so they're bound by it,

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:47.620
just as much as Amazon is,
Amazon just had the foresight,

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.470
to write the contract that they wanted,

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:52.530
so, let's talk about if Parler
was in breach of the contract

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:55.830
and really these kinds of
fights are won and lost,

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.410
based on what's in the contract

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.650
not based on generally the
action that is undertaken,

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:02.280
after the contract is signed,

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.760
so, you can probably guess
how this is gonna turn out,

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:06.760
so, we have to ask, did
Parler breach the agreement?

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.250
And the answer to that is probably simple,

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.810
they hosted a ton of content
that advocated violence,

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:13.300
against a variety of individuals,

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.400
along with other notable illegal content

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.367
and they repeatedly failed to remove it

00:13:17.367 --> 00:13:19.220
but Parler's only mission,

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:22.040
there was a backlog of
26,000 reports of content,

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:23.920
that violated its community standards,

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:26.400
yet remained on the
website and as a result,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.910
individuals were able to
insight and coordinate,

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:32.240
various aspects of the January
6th attack on the US Capitol.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:33.830
- Boom, lawyer.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:35.720
- And what a lot of people think is worse

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:38.100
is that following the horrific event,

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:39.480
the violent and illegal content,

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.280
actually seemed to increase on Parler

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.625
with no sign of slowing down
and section 4.2 makes clear,

00:13:44.625 --> 00:13:46.687
that the customer is responsible,

00:13:46.687 --> 00:13:50.217
"ensure that their content
and their end users use

00:13:50.217 --> 00:13:52.237
"of their content or
the service offerings,

00:13:52.237 --> 00:13:55.807
"will not violate any of the
policies or any applicable law,

00:13:55.807 --> 00:13:57.907
"the customer is quote solely responsible

00:13:57.907 --> 00:14:01.087
"for the development content
operation maintenance and use

00:14:01.087 --> 00:14:02.000
"of its content."

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.620
And in addition section 4.5
of the customer agreement,

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.110
states that if you become
aware of any violation

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.070
of your obligations under this agreement,

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:10.420
caused by an end user,

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:13.150
you will immediately suspend
access to your content

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.770
and the service offerings
by such end user,

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.630
so, what obligations may those be?

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:20.200
Well, the agreement itself states,

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:22.720
that the customer's responsible
to immediately remove

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.080
and suspend end users and
content that poses security risk,

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:28.480
to the service offerings
or any third party.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.755
- Appendix three, soft limits.

00:14:30.755 --> 00:14:31.588
- With you.

00:14:31.588 --> 00:14:33.060
- And in addition as
part of the agreement,

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:35.990
Parler agreed to abide by
Amazon's acceptance use policy,

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:37.710
which prohibits certain content,

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.667
including the content that
"violates the rights of others

00:14:40.667 --> 00:14:42.650
"or that may be harmful to others,"

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:46.180
so the question is was
there a breach of contract?

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.280
Well, based on all of
the available evidence,

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.870
it certainly seems so,

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:53.110
it's just that the breach of
contract was not by Amazon,

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:55.200
it was probably by Parler in that,

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.600
they had recklessly violated
the terms of the agreement,

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:01.720
which resulted in a what seems
like justifiable suspension

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.020
of those services

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:05.000
and finally, that takes
us to the third claim

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.960
in the complaint for
intentional interference

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.560
with prospective economic advantage,

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:11.080
Parler's basis for this claim

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.600
is that a termination of
Parler's account with Amazon,

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:15.800
will intentionally interfere
with the contracts,

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:18.030
Parler has with its present users

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:20.660
as well as the users it
was projected to gain,

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.020
while their account was suspended,

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:25.220
now, a successful claim under this tort,

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.540
requires the establishment
of several elements,

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.450
one, the existence of a valid
contractual relationship

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:31.780
or business expectancy,

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.330
two, the defendant's knowledge
of that relationship,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.300
three, an intentional interference,

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.680
inducing or causing a
breach or termination

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:40.470
of the relationship or expectancy,

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:43.180
four, the defendant's interference
with an improper purpose

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:46.220
or by improper means and
five, results in damage

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:47.740
and here Parler argued that Amazon

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.900
was well aware of their current growth

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:51.940
and the potential for even more growth,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:53.960
highlighted by negotiations with Amazon,

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:55.860
to increase server capacity

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:57.810
and to capitalize on the speculation,

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.410
that president Trump was
going to join Parler,

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:03.270
after being kicked off of
Twitter and Parler argued,

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.350
that this interference was
intentional and improper

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:09.530
and used Parler's purported
breach as just a pretext,

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:11.610
to ensure the Parler wouldn't gain steam,

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:13.420
just at the time they
were likely to experience,

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:15.130
a significant amount of growth

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:17.860
but yet again Parler
leaves out the key facts,

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.060
that are almost indisputable,

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:21.040
that easily negate these allegations,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.180
especially as it pertains
to the fourth element,

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:26.840
interference for an improper
purpose or improper means,

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:28.600
interference claims cannot be made,

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.430
where the alleged interference actions,

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:33.210
were just simply exercising
of a contractual right,

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.980
that someone actually
has it has to be improper

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:39.240
and if you have the contractual
right to do something,

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:42.510
that can't give rise to the
tort of intentional interference

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:45.540
and since Amazon as we've
already made abundantly clear,

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:48.030
had the contractual right
to terminate this contract,

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:52.430
basically immediately an improper
means can't be identified

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.600
as far as an improper purpose,

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.820
Amazon points out in it's reply that,

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:58.960
it would require Amazon to
have acted out of greed,

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:00.810
retaliation or hostility,

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:02.600
none of which was specifically alleged

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.110
in Parler's complaint.

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:06.040
Now, some of the facts go both ways

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:09.110
in fact kicking Parler
off of Amazon's platform,

00:17:09.110 --> 00:17:10.650
actually hurts Amazon as well

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.610
because they'd be foregoing
the revenue from Parler

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:15.890
and there are other things
that go both ways as well,

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.140
like the fact that Amazon
was willing to assist them,

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.100
by helping Parler migrate their services

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:23.320
to a different provider and
didn't hold back the data,

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:25.830
that was presumably rightfully Parler's

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:28.020
in a letter notifying
Parler of their suspension,

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.427
Amazon stated "we will
ensure that all of your data

00:17:30.427 --> 00:17:32.817
"is preserved for you to
migrate to your own servers

00:17:32.817 --> 00:17:34.677
"and we'll work with you as best we can,

00:17:34.677 --> 00:17:36.340
"to help your migration."

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:38.570
Now, those are Parler's three claims

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:40.320
but there's another aspect of this,

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:42.160
that I think Parler's
mentioned at this point,

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.480
which is that Amazon has
certain affirmative defenses,

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.570
just because people make
accusations against you,

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:47.780
doesn't mean that you can't use,

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:49.100
what's called an affirmative defense,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:51.260
to throw out those claims,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.520
even if those claims happen to be true

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:58.520
and with so many things these
days this involves section 230

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.680
of the communications decency act

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:04.470
and here much to the ire of
Trump supporters everywhere,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:06.230
Amazon also argued that

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:09.300
in addition to all of the other
reasons that they provided,

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:11.460
Parler's claims just
fail on their own rights,

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.770
Amazon is also protected by section 230,

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.560
this section explicitly
restricts liability,

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.430
against online service
providers in certain instances,

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.970
when they block perceived harmful content,

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:24.767
section 230 (c)(2)a
specifically states that,

00:18:24.767 --> 00:18:28.027
"no provider or user of an
interactive computer service,

00:18:28.027 --> 00:18:30.527
"shall be held liable on
account of any action,

00:18:30.527 --> 00:18:33.557
"voluntarily taken in good
faith to restrict access to

00:18:33.557 --> 00:18:35.857
"or availability of
material that the provider

00:18:35.857 --> 00:18:39.407
"or user considers to be obscene,
lewd, lascivious, filthy,

00:18:39.407 --> 00:18:42.667
"excessively violent, harassing
or otherwise objectionable,

00:18:42.667 --> 00:18:46.660
"whether or not such material
is constitutionally protected"

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:49.860
this good Samaritan rule then
provides an additional layer

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.550
of protection for Amazon as
a computer service company,

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:55.070
tasked with the tremendous responsibility

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:59.240
of trying to provide a platform
for informational resource

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:00.250
and political discourse,

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.320
while being cognizant of the
need to protect individuals,

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:06.220
against harmfulness information
and illegal activity

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:07.960
and as we've talked about
on this channel before

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:11.060
and we'll probably talk about
many times in the future,

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:14.400
section 230 was a response to
the early days of the internet

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:18.730
where if a website did any
kind of moderation whatsoever,

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:21.760
they were deemed to be
constructively knowledgeable about,

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.160
all of the illegal stuff
that was on their website

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:28.720
and so it was a way to allow
websites to do some moderation,

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:31.860
without being liable for
everything that's on their website,

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:34.800
this applies to Amazon,
it applies to YouTube,

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.710
it applies to anyone that even has a blog,

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:40.420
that has comments on it

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:43.410
and if you didn't have
this kind of protection,

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:45.760
odds are they wouldn't host anything on it

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:47.240
because they'd be too worried about,

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:49.210
all of the illegal stuff
that's hosted on there,

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.550
so, it's either an all
or nothing proposition,

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:54.320
where there would be basically
a hundred percent moderation

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:57.300
and nothing even remotely controversial

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:59.070
or zero moderation at all

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:01.180
and it would just be a complete Wild West.

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.870
So as you probably know the
way Parler started this lawsuit

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:07.570
was a request for a TRO or a
temporary restraining order,

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:10.940
it's an emergency motion to get
someone to not do something,

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:13.390
it started as a way to
try and prevent Amazon

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.530
from kicking Parler off its platform

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:18.650
and then it was basically
an order to demand,

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:22.920
that Amazon put the app
back on its platform

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:25.240
and you can imagine under
some of these circumstances,

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:27.150
where a TRO might be granted

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:30.280
because kicking an app off of a platform,

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:32.800
might give rise to the kind
of irreparable damages,

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:36.670
that a TRO is meant to solve
it's a very temporary thing,

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:38.910
so, you can get to the
merits of the litigation,

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:42.750
without complete destruction
that cannot be undone

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.830
but to get a temporary restraining order,

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:46.660
a plaintiff would have
to establish number one,

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:48.350
that it's likely to succeed on the merits

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:50.260
and as we've just talked about,

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:51.840
that's definitely not going to happen,

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:55.140
all three of its claims pretty
much fail pretty easily,

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:56.590
number two, that you have to show,

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.500
that you're likely to
suffer irreparable harm

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:00.900
in the absence of preliminary relief,

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:04.290
which in this circumstance
probably exists,

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:06.240
having the app completely down,

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.130
the website completely destroyed

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.620
and needing to be rewritten
for some other platform,

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:13.520
yeah, that could be irreparable harm,

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:16.320
number three, the balance of
the equities tips in its favor,

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:18.330
which definitely doesn't exist here

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:21.620
because you can have all the
irreparable damage in the world

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:24.890
but if you're not going
to succeed on the merits,

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:26.720
you're not entitled to any relief

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:28.330
if you're not going to succeed

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:30.020
and finally, the fourth element

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:32.210
is an injunction would be
in the public interest,

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:34.680
which also for basically the same reasons,

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:36.040
doesn't really exist here,

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:39.190
now, that being said, this is
not the end of the lawsuit,

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:42.780
contrary to a lot of media
reporting about this lawsuit,

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:45.540
it's not over by any
stretch of the imagination

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.530
in fact, this is really just
the end of the first inning,

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:50.700
Parler took a big swing they whiffed,

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:53.090
the court didn't grant
the emergency motions

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:54.400
for a temporary restraining order

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:58.180
or the preliminary injunction
but the lawsuit still exists

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:01.040
and the court is still
going to have to adjudicate,

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.920
these claims on the merit of the claims

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:05.930
and while some preliminary evidence

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:08.460
was provided to the court,

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:10.490
we haven't gotten to
the discovery portion,

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:13.860
where they can get a whole
bunch of more evidence,

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:16.210
so, this lawsuit isn't going anywhere,

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:18.637
generally if you lose the
temporary restraining order

00:22:18.637 --> 00:22:21.230
and the court says you're
